the morning a mother or  father said good morning honey where you  on the mood meter you're in the red this  morning why what's going on well mommy  I'm feeling anxious about the test today  my goodness you're anxious Wow let's  think about some strategies that we go  to school together to help you decrease  that anxiety how many of you would like  that to happen for your students I think  it would be wonderful wouldn't it what  we know is that when you involve  everyone you're enhancing  social-emotional skills of individuals  but also shifting the culture and  climate a shared language and finally  what we now know is that great things  happen in terms of outcomes so that's  we're going to wrap up with we know that  schools that have adopted ruler have  students with greater emotional  intelligence less anxiety and depression  fewer attention learning problems higher  academic performance that are problem  solvers  in terms of that climate we've shown up  to a 17% shift in classroom climate what  we show is that the emotional climate is  the initial driver of that change and by  the second year the instructional skills  of teachers become even better  so in conclusion I'm going to ask you  like I asked you in the beginning how  are you feeling everyone please sit up  straight in your seats take a nice long  inhale exhale how many feel a little  different than you did when you came  this morning raise your hand okay how  many of you feeling slightly more  pleasant I'm not going to ask the other  one like it it close your eyes for a  minute and imagine that you're going to  leave here today and text message  someone and you're going to tell them  the one thing that's a take-home message  for you about emotional intelligence and  social-emotional learning what are you  gonna say what's the one thing that  resonates with you from this  presentation your one take-home message  ok please open your eyes in conclusion  as an academic and our team here as a  team of academics and people who are  involved in the practice of emotional  intelligence we can't make audacious  claims about our work we can only say  what our research shows and what the  anecdotes come in from what the  antidotes say as they come in from our  thousands of schools now hundreds of  schools around the globe but James  Heckman I think said it best Nobel  laureate in economics he said the  greatest returns and education  investments are from nurturing  children's non cognitive skills  I don't love the term non cognitive  skills because I do think of emotional  intelligence being a very cognitive  skill it's a very hard skill but we need  to give children the social emotional  and behavior benefits that lead to later  success in life so my final words are I  hope that we're in agreement that the  time has come at least in Connecticut to  take the social and emotional education  of his children seriously thank you very  much  you
 

